    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d496">

                <pb facs="0613" n="603" sameAs="frus:frus1948v07/pg603"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/11–2248: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State
                        to the Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Stuart</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1948-11-23T19:00:00-05:00">November 23,
                            1948—7 p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1690. Dept. appreciative statement urtel 2279 Nov 22 neither you nor members your
                    staff have advised or promoted coalition Govt with Comm participation in
                    conversations with ChiGovt or polit leaders.</p>
                <p>Re suggestion Urtels 2220 and 2279 for statement US position Comm participation
                    in ChiGovt, you should note Deptel 1164 Aug 12 which authorized you informally
                    indicate US Govt attitude as implying, whether directly or indirectly, no
                    support, encouragement or acceptability coalition Govt in China with Comm
                    participation. In event you consider desirable from standpoint US interests, you
                    may in discussions with local leaders orally and informally indicate US Govt
                    does not favor Comm participation in Govts China or elsewhere in world. Pres
                    statement Mar 10 [<hi rendition="#italic">11</hi>]<anchor n="54"
                        xml:id="d496-f1"/> should have made this sufficiently clear. Careful
                    consideration being given here issuance statement on US Govt position toward
                    China as being suggested from many sources both in China and in US. It will be
                    realized that such statement may not have effect desired by many who urge such
                    action.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lovett</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d496-f1" n="54" xml:id="d496-f1-content">
                <p>See <hi rendition="#italic">United States Relations With China</hi>, pp.
